📘 Developer Guide


Operating System: Rocky Linux 10
Project Name: Process Monitoring & Visualization Tool

1. Overview

This project provides a lightweight process-monitoring system composed of:

A Bash parsing script (parse_prc.sh) that extracts raw process data from /proc.

A Python backend & GUI (gui.py + parsing_engine.py) that visualizes the parsed process information for end users.

The entire system is operated through a single command:

python3 gui.py

2. File Structure & Responsibilities
2.1 parse_prc.sh

Role: Process data extractor

Description:

Reads process information directly from /proc/<pid>.

Extracts the following fields:

Elapsed Time

PID

User Name

Memory Usage (RSS in kB and %)

Parent PID (PPid)

Executable Path

Command Line

Outputs each record in a pipe-delimited format (|) to ensure consistent parsing.

Characteristics:

Does not rely on external tools (pure /proc-based).

Handles missing or invalid values gracefully ([Unknown]).

Designed for stability even under high system load.

2.2 parsing_engine.py

Role: Parsing coordinator

Description:

Automatically executes parse_prc.sh.

Converts the script’s pipe-delimited output into structured process entries.

Performs lightweight preprocessing and validation.

Supplies parsed results to the GUI.

Notes:

Contains no business logic related to GUI.

Accessed only by gui.py.

2.3 gui.py

Role: Main execution entry point + Visualization frontend

Description:

Provides an interactive GUI for viewing process data.

Displays each parsed process as a table row.

Offers basic user conveniences such as:

Data refresh

Sorting

Visual organization of fields

Important:

This is the only file the user executes.

Automatically manages all interaction with parsing_engine.py.

3. System Workflow
[User] → runs gui.py
gui.py → requests data from parsing_engine.py
parsing_engine.py → executes parse_prc.sh
parse_prc.sh → reads /proc and outputs '|' delimited rows
gui.py → displays the processed output

4. Output Format Specification

parse_prc.sh outputs rows in the following strict format:

<Elapsed>|<PID>|<USER>|<MEM_KB>|<MEM_%>|<PPID>|<PATH>|<CMDLINE>


This ensures:

No dependency on whitespace

No interference from long command lines

Consistent parsing across all environments

5. Installation & Execution
Requirements

Rocky Linux 10

Bash 4+

Python 3.9+

Setup
chmod +x parse_prc.sh

Run Application
python3 gui.py

6. Error Handling & Stability
In parse_prc.sh

Missing /proc/<pid>/exe → [Unknown]

Empty cmdline → fallback to process name

Invalid elapsed time → [Unknown]

Missing memory metrics → safe defaults

In parsing_engine.py

Malformed lines skipped safely

No dependency on tabs or whitespace

In gui.py

Displays “No Data” if script output is empty

GUI will not crash due to malformed entries

7. Extensibility

Developers can extend:

Shell script:

Add CPU ticks / CPU usage

Count open file descriptors

Add thread count

Engine:

Summary statistics

Result filtering

GUI:

Sorting buttons

Search filters

Memory usage charts

8. Notes for Maintainability

Do not change the output delimiter (|).

Ensure compatibility with /proc layout on Linux 4.x+.

Keep parsing logic minimal and forward-compatible.

9. Execution Summary

Only one command is needed:

python3 gui.py


Everything else runs automatically in the background.
